diff --git a/CLAUDE.md b/CLAUDE.md
index 1aa337a1..0ac64e1f 100644
--- a/CLAUDE.md
+++ b/CLAUDE.md
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@ Respond to the user in **Traditional Taiwanese Chinese (繁體中文 / 台灣用
 - **Implementation**: once the plan is clear, use **`/tdd`** to drive the work through the red-green-refactor loop rather than writing implementation first.
 - **Verifying a change**: run the **targeted** tests for what you touched, plus `ruff check` / `ruff format --check` / `ty check`. That is the whole of the per-change gate — **do not run the full suite locally**, not even CI's `-m "not integration"` subset. The 100% `coverage` gate in Commands below is a **release / on-request** gate, not a per-change one.
 - **A PR the operator must act on carries its own entry in `docs/migrations.md`, in the same PR.** That file is the operator's upgrade runbook — one entry per PR, in master merge order, oldest first, `### YYYY-MM-DD · <merge sha> · #N title {#pr-N}` (the merge sha may be left out before merge; date and PR number may not), four boxes (設定 / 資料 / k8s · CI 側 / 確認做完) and, for every action, the command, WHEN it runs (rollout 前 / 後 / 擋 readiness), WHY in one or two sentences, and the symptom of skipping it. An entry is required when the PR: bumps a `Schema(Model, "vN")` version anywhere under `src/` (grep, not just `resources/__init__.py`); removes, moves or renames a config key (the loader rejects unknown keys, so this is "refuses to boot"); changes a default or adds a knob whose default changes behaviour or cost; adds a JobType, a k8s manifest, a probe or an env the deploy must add; or changes behaviour with no knob at all. A purely opt-in knob gets a line in `docs/configuration.md`, not here. There is no CI guard for this — the rule is the guard: the runbook once fell two months behind and eight `source-doc` bumps were never recorded, so "I'll add it later" is how it drifts. Never re-add a per-model summary table or a second list in `deployment.md`; two lists drift apart (they did).
+- **A plan that overturns an earlier plan marks it, in the same PR.** When a new plan reverses a decision recorded in an older `plan-*.md`, add `> 被 #NNN（plan-new.md）推翻` as the old plan's first line under its title. The old plan is not rewritten: an AI that reads both can tell which is current; the one failure is reading the old one alone, and this line is what leads it to the new one — the in-app AI reads the docs through the `system-help` skill (`docs/plan-ai-reads-docs.md`). The format and the target file are guarded (`tests/docs/test_docs_index.py`); whether a plan overturns another is not, so the rule is the guard. Every new plan also gets a line in `docs/design-history.md`, and every other new doc a place in `mkdocs.yml`'s `nav` — the same test fails the PR otherwise.
 - **Phase numbering**: plans use a **flat integer sequence** — `Phase 1`, `Phase 2`, … (`P1`, `P2`, …). **Never** sub-phases like `Phase 1a` / `Phase 1b`. "Phase 1" means Phase 1 is to be *completed*; if work is split off, it becomes the next integer (`Phase 2`), not a letter suffix.
 - **Review first, CI last (2026-09-18).** CI runs only after the adversarial review round has come back clean — not in parallel with it. The old rule ("let CI start early, cancel it on a finding") meant every round of review cancelled a 20–30 min run that was grading a state about to be replaced, and a green light seen mid-review answered nothing. So: push as soon as the seconds-long gates pass (`ruff check`, `ruff format --check`, `ty check`) — the push is the backup of the work and what opens/updates the draft PR — but **cancel the CI run that push triggers** (`gh run list --branch <b>` → `gh run cancel <id>`; `ci.yml` fires on `pull_request`, so a PR branch cannot push without starting one). Run the review lenses (conformance / veracity / defect / regression, in parallel, each in its own worktree). Fix, commit, push, cancel again, re-review as the fix shape demands. When a round comes back **clean**, start CI on the final sha (`gh run rerun <id>` for that sha, or the run the last push started), let it finish, and report on it. Never report CI green while anything is unpushed: a green light answers "is it ready", so it must describe the final state. Local verification stays targeted (the per-change gate above); the full suite is CI's.
 - **Review and CI answer different questions, so run both — and believe the review.** CI only runs the tests that already exist, and defects live in the code nobody wrote a test for. A green CI is a floor, not a verdict; an adversarial review round is the verdict. A round with a finding: fix, then decide whether the fix itself needs another round — one that **replaces a mechanism** is new code and does; a comment or a one-line fix with a test pinning it does not.
